### Workload Proof Token (WPT)
* **Presenter**: Brian Campbell
* **Slides**: [Workload Proof Token](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-wimse-sessa-workload-proof-token-01)
* **Document**: `draft-ietf-wimse-wpt`
* Recent updates include moving the Workload Proof Token (WPT) into the HTTP `Authorization` header field (instead of a dedicated header) and updating algorithm identifiers to use ED25519 (`Ed25519`).
* **Discussion on Header Placement**:
  * Flemming Andreasen expressed concern that placing WPT exclusively in the `Authorization` header limits composition with other authentication credentials (e.g., OAuth access tokens) and questioned consistency with `draft-ietf-wimse-arch` and HTTP message signature approaches.
  * Jonathan Rosenberg and Jeff Lombardo raised use cases where an AI agent acting on behalf of a user needs to send an end-user OAuth access token alongside an agent proof.
  * Brian Campbell clarified that WPT is intended primarily for backend service-to-service calls within a trust domain, where context can be passed via transaction tokens or separate mechanisms rather than combining multiple credentials in the `Authorization` header.
  * The chairs requested that technical debate on `Authorization` header exclusivity be continued on the mailing list.

